---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/dna-heredity">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Biology. It needs zero prior knowledge. The
    only everyday tool it asks of you is one you already own: you have
    noticed that children look like their parents — and that no child is an
    exact copy. This lesson is about the instructions behind that fact.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what DNA is, and explain why its pairing rule lets it be copied;</li>
    <li>use the words chromosome, gene and allele correctly;</li>
    <li>predict a simple offspring ratio with a Punnett square;</li>
    <li>explain why brothers and sisters are all different;</li>
    <li>say what a mutation is, and why most mutations never reach your children.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start with a family resemblance</h2>
  <p>
    Maybe you have your mother's laugh, or your father's curly hair. Children
    resemble their parents — but never exactly. Something must carry the
    instructions from parent to child. That something is
    <strong>DNA</strong>: a molecule shaped like a two-stranded ladder
    twisted into a spiral. You began as a single cell holding one complete
    copy. You now have roughly 30 trillion cells, and every one runs on a
    copy of the same library.
  </p>
  <p>
    The ladder's rungs come in four kinds, written A, T, G and C. And the
    rungs obey one strict rule: <strong>A pairs only with T, and G only with
    C</strong>. That rule changes everything, because it means each strand is
    a complete template for the other. Unzip the ladder, and either side can
    rebuild its missing partner. Copying, repairing and reading the molecule
    all rest on this one rule.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now the scale. Your whole set of instructions — your
    <strong>genome</strong> — is about 3 billion rungs. Printed as a book of
    single letters, it would fill a thousand volumes. It is spread across 23
    pairs of chromosomes and contains roughly 20,000 protein-coding genes
    (both words defined just below). How humanity worked all this out is a
    150-year detective story — scrub through it below, then we will unpack
    each step.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="call the magnitude — your genome in print" widget="estimate" config={dhVolumesEstimate} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="from monk's garden to programmable editor — 150 years in ten steps" widget="timeline" config={dnaTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> scrub the timeline from Mendel's peas
    in 1866 to CRISPR in 2012 and watch the evidence build in layers —
    first the carrier of heredity is hunted down (Griffith, Avery,
    Hershey–Chase), then the molecule itself is read (the double helix, the
    code), then it becomes a tool (PCR, CRISPR). Notice how long each step
    took: Mendel's paper was ignored for 34 years, and the whole structure
    still rested on one X-ray photograph.
  </p>

  <h2>Chromosomes, genes and the recipe book</h2>
  <p>
    Your DNA in one cell, stretched out straight, would run about 2 metres.
    So it is packed, wound around spool-like proteins, into 46 compact
    <strong>chromosomes</strong> — 23 matched <strong>pairs</strong>, one
    chromosome of each pair from your mother and one from your father. A
    full double set like this is called <em>diploid</em>.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="fill the blanks — packing the library" widget="cloze" config={dhPackCloze} />
  <p>
    A <strong>gene</strong> is a section of DNA that codes for one protein,
    sitting at a fixed address on a chromosome. Both chromosomes of a pair
    carry that address, so you hold two copies of most genes. Alternative
    versions of the same gene — the brown-eye version and the blue-eye
    version, say — are <strong>alleles</strong>.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each word with what it names" widget="match" config={dhTermsMatch} />
  <p>
    Reading the library goes like this. The four bases are an alphabet.
    Three letters at a time spell one amino acid. Chains of amino acids fold
    into proteins that do the cell's work. That is the whole reading scheme,
    and it is the same in bacteria, blue whales and bread wheat.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="rebuild the reading scheme" widget="order" config={dhCodeOrder} />
  <p>
    Watson and Crick's 1953 paper mattered because the structure explained
    the copying in one glance — pairing rule plus two strands is a copying
    machine. They announced it in one of science's great understatements:
    "It has not escaped our notice that the specific pairing we have
    postulated immediately suggests a possible copying mechanism for the
    genetic material." The structure itself leaned on Rosalind Franklin's
    X-ray image "Photo 51", which measured the helix's diameter and the
    spacing of its turns; history has been correcting her credit ever since.
    By 1966, Nirenberg, Khorana and colleagues had read the code outright —
    all 64 triplets — and found that nearly all life speaks this exact
    dialect, the strongest single clue that everything alive shares one
    ancestor.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> DNA is a ladder whose rungs pair by rule, packed
    into chromosome pairs, with genes at fixed addresses. Next: how cells
    copy the library — and how parents hand half of it to children.
  </p>

  <h2>Copying the library: mitosis, briefly</h2>
  <p>
    Growth and repair need new cells that carry the full, unchanged library.
    The copying division is <strong>mitosis</strong>: the DNA is replicated,
    and each new cell receives a complete double set. The replication is
    <em>semi-conservative</em> — each new ladder keeps one old strand and
    builds one new one — confirmed in a famous 1958 experiment by Meselson
    and Stahl. Errors do slip through (about one letter per few hundred
    million copied), and most are caught and repaired. The rare escapees are
    mutations, the raw material of both disease and evolution — more on them
    below.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — copying the library" widget="judge" config={dhRepJudge} />

  <h2>Halving the deck: meiosis</h2>
  <p>
    Making children has a counting problem. Each child gets one chromosome
    of every pair from each parent. If parents handed over full double sets,
    the count would double every generation — 46 chromosomes becoming 92,
    then 184, and nonsense within a century. The solution is
    <strong>meiosis</strong>: a special two-stage division that makes the sex
    cells — <strong>gametes</strong>, meaning sperm and eggs — carrying a
    single set of 23 chromosomes. Fertilisation puts two single sets back
    together into 46.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="find the faulty line — the counting problem" widget="spot-error" config={dhMeiosisSpot} />
  <p>
    Here is the beautiful part: your gametes are all different. In the first
    meiotic division, the chromosome pairs line up and are dealt to the two
    ends independently of their neighbours. That shuffle alone lets one
    human make over 8 million (2<sup>23</sup>) genetically different gametes.
    On top of that, matched chromosomes physically swap segments —
    <strong>crossing over</strong> — creating chromosomes that never existed
    before. Variation is not a bug of sex; it is the entire point.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> gametes are not "half a person" of each parent in any
  simple sense. Each egg or sperm carries a randomised mix of your mother's and father's
  versions — which is why full siblings (other than identical twins) share only about half
  their variable DNA, and why you are not 50% your mother and 50% your father gene by gene,
  but a fresh reshuffle every time.</p>

  <h2>Mendel's ratios, explained</h2>
  <p>
    In the 1850s, Gregor Mendel, a monk growing peas, crossed pure tall
    plants with pure short ones. Every offspring was tall. He let those
    hybrids self-fertilise — and the short trait reappeared, in a stubborn
    <strong>3:1</strong> ratio across thousands of plants. His explanation,
    published in 1866 and ignored for 34 years: each plant carries two
    copies of every gene. An allele can be <strong>dominant</strong>
    (visible whenever it is present) or <strong>recessive</strong> (visible
    only in a double dose). The tall parents were TT; the short ones tt; the
    hybrids all Tt — tall, because T dominates.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now cross two hybrids, Tt with Tt. Each parent passes on T or t with
    equal chance, and the outcomes fan out into a grid called a
    <strong>Punnett square</strong>:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="one cross, four outcomes — click each square to read its genotype" widget="diagram" config={punnettCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> click each of the four outcome squares
    and read its genotype and probability — one TT, two Tt, one tt. That is
    the genotype ratio 1:2:1, yet three of the four squares are tall, which
    is exactly where the 3:1 phenotype ratio comes from. Click a Tt square
    again and note the hidden t: a tall plant can still be carrying short.
  </p>

  <p>
    Read the grid: one TT, two Tt, one tt. That is the <em>genotype</em>
    ratio 1:2:1 — the actual allele combinations. But the two Tt plants look
    identical, so what you <em>see</em> — the <em>phenotype</em> — is 3 tall
    to 1 short. The arithmetic is simple probability, and it works the same
    in humans: two carriers of the recessive allele for cystic fibrosis have,
    with each pregnancy, a 1-in-4 chance of an affected child. Inheritance
    of one gene like this is called <strong>monohybrid inheritance</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two wrinkles. Blood groups: A and B are <em>codominant</em> (an AB person
    shows both) while O is recessive, so an A-parent and a B-parent can have
    children of every group. And most human traits — height, skin tone — are
    <em>polygenic</em>: thousands of genes acting together, which is why they
    come in smooth ranges rather than neat 3:1 ratios. Mendel's peas were, in
    a sense, lucky choices of clean traits.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="file the traits by their inheritance pattern" widget="sort-buckets" config={dhTraitsBuckets} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> two copies of every gene, one from each parent;
    dominant beats recessive; a Tt cross gives 3 tall to 1 short. Next: what
    happens when the copy itself changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Mutations: when the copy changes</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>mutation</strong> is a change in the DNA sequence itself — a
    base substituted, deleted or inserted during copying, or a whole
    chromosome reshuffled. Mutations arise spontaneously, because copying is
    near-perfect but not perfect. They are multiplied by
    <strong>mutagens</strong>, things that damage DNA: UV light in sunlight,
    ionising radiation, chemicals in tobacco smoke. Most mutations are
    silent or harmful; occasionally one is useful — and that mix of mostly
    noise with occasional signal is the raw material evolution edits.
  </p>
  <p>
    The sickle-cell mutation shows the whole spectrum in one allele. One DNA
    letter swapped, one amino acid changed in haemoglobin, and red blood
    cells warp into inflexible crescents. Two copies cause sickle-cell
    disease, serious and lifelong. One copy — the carrier state — does
    something remarkable: it protects against malaria. That is why the
    allele persists at high frequency precisely where malaria is common. A
    mutation's meaning depends on the environment it meets: that sentence is
    genetics, evolution and medicine in eleven words.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a mutation in one of your body's cells changes the DNA your children will inherit. Only mutations in the gametes — or the germline cells that make them — pass to the next generation; the mutations that turn a sunburnt skin cell cancerous die with that skin. This is why mutagens harm you personally, but harm your lineage only when they reach the germline — a distinction that radiation safety, cancer medicine and evolution all depend on.</p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="trace the mutation — who inherits it?" widget="branch" config={dhMutationBranch} />

  <h2>Sex-linked inheritance: a worked cross</h2>
  <p>
    Colour-blindness rides the X chromosome. Call the normal version of the
    gene X<sup>N</sup> and the colour-blind version X<sup>c</sup>. A carrier
    mother (X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>c</sup>, sighted) and a normal father
    (X<sup>N</sup>Y) produce four equally likely outcomes: daughters
    X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>N</sup> (sighted) or X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>c</sup>
    (carrier), sons X<sup>N</sup>Y (sighted) or X<sup>c</sup>Y
    (colour-blind). A son gets his only X from his mother, so each son has a
    1-in-2 chance of colour-blindness, while each daughter has a 1-in-2
    chance of carrying it silently. A colour-blind father cannot pass it to
    his son at all — the son got the Y. Haemophilia follows the same
    arithmetic; both conditions were tracked through European royal
    pedigrees, in one of history's unintended genetic experiments.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pedigree charts</strong> — circles for females, squares for
    males, shading for affected — let you reason backwards. Two unaffected
    parents with an affected child prove the condition is recessive: both
    parents must be carriers. An affected mother passing it to <em>all</em>
    her sons and none of her daughters points to the X chromosome. An
    affected father passing it to an affected son rules out X-linkage
    entirely. Reading a pedigree is the Punnett square run in reverse — and
    it is exactly how genetic counsellors estimate a real family's risk
    before a pregnancy.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> dominant does not mean "stronger" or "more common" — it
  means the allele shows when only one copy is present. Huntington's disease is dominant and
  rare; blue eyes are recessive and common in northern Europe. Dominance describes
  <em>expression</em>, not strength or frequency.</p>

  <h2>Genes are not destiny: the PKU lesson</h2>
  <p>
    One metabolic condition holds the whole nature–nurture debate in a test
    tube. <strong>Phenylketonuria (PKU)</strong> is a recessive disorder:
    children with two defective copies of one enzyme gene cannot break down
    the amino acid phenylalanine, which builds up in the blood and damages
    the developing brain — untreated, it used to cause severe intellectual
    disability. But since the 1960s a simple blood test at birth spots it,
    and a diet low in phenylalanine produces completely normal development.
    The genotype was fixed at conception; the outcome was changed entirely
    by environment. That is why modern genetics writes statements like
    "increases risk by 20%" — the honest summary of the field being that
    genes load, environment aims, and the two are only separable in textbook
    diagrams.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>DNA is a ladder with four kinds of rung. State the pairing rule, and say why it makes copying possible.</li>
    <li>Cross Tt with Tt. What fraction of offspring is short, and why?</li>
    <li>A man is colour-blind. Can he pass the allele to his son? To a daughter?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) A pairs only with T and G only with C — so
    each strand is a complete template, and either side can rebuild its
    partner. (2) One in four: the grid gives TT, Tt, Tt, tt, and only tt
    (double dose of the recessive allele) is short. (3) Not to his son — the
    son receives his Y chromosome. Possibly to a daughter, who would be a
    sighted carrier like his own mother.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>DNA's pairing rule — A with T, G with C — makes each strand a template, so the molecule can be copied and read.</li>
    <li>DNA is packed into 23 pairs of chromosomes; a gene sits at a fixed address, and alleles are alternative versions of the same gene.</li>
    <li>Mitosis copies the full library for growth and repair; meiosis halves it for gametes, shuffling and crossing over so every gamete differs.</li>
    <li>In a Tt cross with Tt, the genotype ratio is 1:2:1 and the phenotype ratio 3:1 — dominant shows in a single dose, recessive only in a double.</li>
    <li>Sex-linked traits ride the X chromosome, which is why recessive conditions such as colour-blindness show most often in genetic males.</li>
    <li>Mutations are changes in the DNA sequence; only those in gametes reach children, and a mutation's effect depends on the environment it meets.</li>
    <li>Genes load, environment aims: PKU shows a genotype fixed at conception with an outcome changed entirely by environment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Heredity is the mechanism the rest of biology runs on. Evolution by
    natural selection edits exactly the variation that meiosis and mutation
    supply here, and classification reads the same DNA to draw the tree of
    life. The cell structure lesson showed the nucleus that archives this
    library; photosynthesis and respiration show the energy economy every
    gene's products work inside.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    One pair of chromosomes deserves special mention. Twenty-two pairs are
    autosomes — matched twins. The 23rd is the sex-determining pair: XX in
    female-typical development, XY in male-typical, with the crucial
    asymmetry that mothers always contribute an X and fathers flip a coin
    between X and Y. The Y is small and carries remarkably little, but one
    gene on it (SRY) triggers the male developmental pathway. Because the X
    carries many ordinary genes, a recessive allele on it has no partner in
    XY individuals — which is why conditions such as red-green
    colour-blindness and haemophilia show up far more often in genetic
    males: a single copy suffices.
  </p>
  <p>
    The library also contains far more than recipes. Only about 2% of your
    DNA codes for proteins; much of the rest was once dismissed as "junk",
    but decades of the ENCODE project and its successors have revealed vast
    tracts of regulatory switches, RNA genes and structural elements — the
    instruction manual's wiring diagram, not its parts list. Meanwhile the
    same library supports the courtroom: DNA fingerprinting reads about 20
    highly variable marker regions, and the odds of two unrelated people
    matching are astronomically small — which is how forensic genetics and
    the Innocence Project's exonerations work. CRISPR, the 2012
    discovery-turned-tool in the timeline, closes the loop: a bacterial
    immune system became programmable molecular scissors, and with it
    heredity turned from a subject of study into an object of editing —
    somatic gene therapies are already approved for sickle-cell disease.
    Whether we edit embryos, and on whose authority, is not a biology
    question; it is the ethics question this century inherited along with
    the code.
  </p>

